;; kotoba.lang.coll -- the GUEST-ROUTE twin of `kotoba-lang/coll`'s
;; `kotoba.lang.coll` (the `.cljk` host library), carrying ONLY its set
;; algebra: `set-union` / `set-intersection` / `set-difference` / `subset?` /
;; `superset?`. A TEMPLATE over the element type, the same text as
;; `lang/compat/clojure/set.kotoba` under coll's names, for the same reason
;; and with the same measured limits. Same dual-runtime shape as
;; `kotoba/lang/text.kotoba` beside this file: nbb keeps loading the host
;; `.cljk`, amu's project route links this `.kotoba` (extension priority
;; across roots -- read that file's header for the pattern).
;;
;; WHY THIS EXISTS (adr-2609111900, the self-host ceiling loop, 2026-09-12):
;; at selfhost-distance measurement 14 (amu 75f2c37, 225 compiler sources on
;; the project route) 18 modules were refused
;;   `variadic function deep-merge is used as a value; a variadic defn is
;;    specialized per call site and has no single interface`
;; -- every one of them inside the DEPENDENCY `kotoba.lang.coll`: coll's own
;; 2 files, plus the 17 `kotoba-lang/security` modules that require it. The
;; host coll is a portable stdlib whose body passes its own variadic
;; `deep-merge` as a value (`(reduce deep-merge ...)`), takes functions as
;; values (`map-vals`, `filter-keys`, the walk family) and grows maps of
;; unknown shape -- host-runtime properties of that module, not admitted as
;; is, and not going to be.
;;
;; What security actually CALLS from coll is exactly the clojure.set algebra
;; under coll's names: `set-difference` (20 call sites), `set-intersection`
;; (7), `set-union` (3) and `subset?` (1), as measured at 14. Re-counted on
;; the security checkout 0e8e1a6 (17 requiring files, alias
;; `[kotoba.lang.coll :as set]`): set-difference 18, subset? 15,
;; set-intersection 6, set-union 4 -- the same four names, and NO other
;; export of coll is called by security at all. So the guest route gets a
;; module of the SAME NAME whose body is admitted and whose surface is those
;; four plus `superset?` (subset? with its arguments swapped, which the host
;; also exports and which clojure/set.kotoba's :absent entry pointed here).
;;
;; WHAT IS FORWARDED, AND FROM WHERE. Nothing is forwarded; the algebra is
;; RESTATED here over the language's typed set, byte-for-byte the four
;; bodies of `clojure/set.kotoba` (union -> set-union, difference ->
;; set-difference, intersection -> set-intersection, subset? -> subset?)
;; plus superset?. Two files, one algebra: a `(:require [clojure.set ...])`
;; and a `(:require [kotoba.lang.coll ...])` cannot both be served by one
;; module name, and a `.kotoba` cannot re-export another module's function
;; under a new name (`(:export [name])` must name a defn of this module,
;; measured 2026-09-08, `:kotoba.error/export-names-undefined`). The oracle
;; that holds the two in agreement is not each other but the HOST
;; `kotoba.lang.coll` itself: `test/kotoba/lang/kotoba_lang_coll_compat_test.cljk`
;; runs this text on the KIR interpreter over every pair of subsets of a
;; five-element universe -- 1,024 pairs per function -- against
;; `coll/set-union` etc., the very functions a `.cljk` author wrote against.
;;
;; THE HOST'S ARITIES, AND WHICH ONE THIS ANSWERS. The host `set-union` is
;; variadic over zero or more sets, `set-intersection` / `set-difference`
;; over one or more (each a `reduce` over its own two-argument clause);
;; `subset?` / `superset?` are two-argument. Only the TWO-ARGUMENT shape is
;; here, exactly as clojure/set.kotoba: this language has no variadic
;; function, and security's call sites are the two-argument shape in all but
;; two places -- one `(set/set-difference required claims stale)` (three
;; arguments: refused here by COUNT at that call site) and one
;; `(reduce set/set-union #{} ...)` (the function as a VALUE: a different
;; wall, the one the linker's qualified-CALL rewrite does not reach). Neither
;; is answered differently; each is refused.
;;
;; The host's one-argument `set-union` answers `(or s1 #{})` and its
;; two-argument arities coerce a nil operand to the empty set; there is no
;; nil here, and an empty typed set is `(typed-set-new [:set elem])`. Where
;; the host `subset?` first compares counts and then walks, this walks and
;; stops at the first member that is not in s2; the SET-valued answers and
;; the booleans are the same on every input both accept, which is what the
;; 1,024-pair sweep asserts.
;;
;; `(:params [elem :keyword])` for the same reason clojure/set.kotoba has
;; it: security's sources are dual-runtime and their bare
;; `(:require [kotoba.lang.coll :as set])` cannot carry a `:with` (measured
;; 2026-09-11: ClojureScript refuses the libspec option, Clojure and nbb
;; ignore it). The default is what security's sets are -- gap ids, gate
;; ids, control ids, claim names, purposes: keyword sets. A call over another
;; element type is refused at the call site, by type:
;; `expression type mismatch: expected [:set :keyword], got [:set :string]`.
;;
;; EVERY OTHER EXPORT of the host coll -- assoc-some, bounded-postwalk,
;; bounded-prewalk, deep-merge, default-max-walk-depth, filter-keys,
;; filter-vals, index, index-by, join, keywordize-keys, map-keys, map-vals,
;; postwalk, postwalk-replace, prewalk, prewalk-replace, project, rename,
;; rename-keys, select, stringify-keys, walk -- is ABSENT with a one-line
;; reason in `lang/compat.edn` (`:modules :kotoba.lang.coll :absent`): each
;; takes a function or an untyped map as a value, or answers a relation, and
;; none of those has a typed spelling here yet. Absent by name, so a caller
;; is refused `required module ... does not export`, never approximated under
;; the real name.
;;
;; Consumed through the ordinary project route, which needs no JVM:
;;   amu check app.cljk --source-path <app> \
;;     --source-path <this repo>/lang/compat --jvm-free
;; Two roots then carry `kotoba.lang.coll` -- `coll/src` the `.cljk`, this
;; directory the `.kotoba` -- and amu's cross-root rule lets the `.kotoba`
;; twin stand in for the `.cljk` (measured for kotoba.lang.text, 2026-09-12;
;; the rule is per namespace, not per file, so it holds here unchanged).

(ns kotoba.lang.coll
  (:params [elem :keyword])
  (:export [set-union set-intersection set-difference subset? superset?]))

;; s1 with every member of s2 conjoined. `conj` reaches the typed set through
;; the friendly surface; `typed-set-nth` walks s2 by index, so this is
;; O(|s2|) steps of a fuel-bounded loop.
(defn set-union [s1 [:set elem] s2 [:set elem]] [:set elem]
  (loop [i 0 acc s1]
    (if (< i (count s2))
      (recur (+ i 1) (conj acc (typed-set-nth [:set elem] s2 i)))
      acc)))

;; The members of s1 that s2 contains, built up from the empty set.
;; `contains?` does not reach a typed set through the friendly surface (it is
;; a map head there), so the primitive is spelled.
(defn set-intersection [s1 [:set elem] s2 [:set elem]] [:set elem]
  (loop [i 0 acc (typed-set-new [:set elem])]
    (if (< i (count s1))
      (let [x (typed-set-nth [:set elem] s1 i)]
        (recur (+ i 1) (if (typed-set-contains [:set elem] s2 x) (conj acc x) acc)))
      acc)))

;; s1 with every member of s2 disjoined. The host walks s1 and keeps what s2
;; does not contain; the SET it answers is the same either way, and this
;; walks s2 unconditionally (a member absent from s1 is a no-op, as `disj`
;; is).
(defn set-difference [s1 [:set elem] s2 [:set elem]] [:set elem]
  (loop [i 0 acc s1]
    (if (< i (count s2))
      (recur (+ i 1) (disj acc (typed-set-nth [:set elem] s2 i)))
      acc)))

;; Every member of s1 is in s2; vacuously true of an empty s1, as the host's
;; subset? is. Stops at the first member that is not.
(defn subset? [s1 [:set elem] s2 [:set elem]] :bool
  (loop [i 0]
    (if (< i (count s1))
      (if (typed-set-contains [:set elem] s2 (typed-set-nth [:set elem] s1 i))
        (recur (+ i 1))
        false)
      true)))

;; Every member of s2 is in s1 -- subset? with its arguments swapped, written
;; out rather than called so that each export stays one defn of this module
;; with its own walk. Vacuously true of an empty s2.
(defn superset? [s1 [:set elem] s2 [:set elem]] :bool
  (loop [i 0]
    (if (< i (count s2))
      (if (typed-set-contains [:set elem] s1 (typed-set-nth [:set elem] s2 i))
        (recur (+ i 1))
        false)
      true)))
